INT. FIELD HOSPITAL TENT - DAY

Cots in rows. Canvas walls glow with afternoon sun. A fan turns slowly overhead.

SERGEANT AUGUSTINE MBEKI (30s, bandaged arm) sits on a cot cleaning his boots. Across the aisle, CORPORAL TADEUSZ KOWALCZYK (20s, head wrapped in gauze) stares at the ceiling. At the foot of Tadeusz's cot, MEDIC FELICITY ROUSE (30s, bloodied apron) writes in a chart.

AUGUSTINE
How's the head?

TADEUSZ
Still attached.

FELICITY
He's lucky. Another inch and it wouldn't be.

AUGUSTINE
You said that about me last week.

FELICITY
And you're still lucky.

TADEUSZ
Is he always like this?

FELICITY
Worse on Tuesdays.

AUGUSTINE
It's Wednesday.

FELICITY
Then you're improving.

She flips a page, checks Tadeusz's pulse, and moves down the row. Augustine watches her go.

AUGUSTINE
She's the best medic in the division.

TADEUSZ
She's the only medic in the division.

AUGUSTINE
That too.

Silence. The fan ticks.

TADEUSZ
Do you think they'll send us back?

AUGUSTINE
They always do.

TADEUSZ
When?

AUGUSTINE
When we're ready.

TADEUSZ
And if we're not?

AUGUSTINE
Then they'll send us anyway.

Tadeusz closes his eyes. Augustine goes back to his boots, brushing off the dust, one stroke at a time.

FELICITY (O.S.)
Mail's here! Anybody expecting a letter?

Neither of them answers.
